{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And Moses took the bones of Joseph with him.</b> This matter is juxtaposed to the verse above, ״וְחֲמֻשִׁים עָלוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 13:18), because it is brought in the Yalkut on this passage that there journeyed in the wilderness two arks — the ark of the Shekhinah and the ark of Joseph — and the nations would ask: What is the nature of the ark of a dead man alongside the ark of the Life of the Worlds? And they would answer them: This one fulfilled what is written of that one, for Joseph fulfilled all Ten Commandments, etc. And this is juxtaposed to what is said ״וְחֲמֻשִׁים עָלוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — armed, and their weapon was the holy ark that went with them in battle. Now after the giving of the Torah this is fine, but before the giving of the Torah, how did they go up armed? Was the holy ark not yet with them! Therefore it says ״וַיִּקַּח מֹשֶׁה אֶת עַצְמוֹת יוֹסֵף עִמּוֹ״, for the ark of Joseph was as though all Ten Commandments were engraved upon it." "Another matter: because Moses saw that HaShem, blessed be He, turned them toward the way of the wilderness of the Sea of Reeds, Moses reasoned that if perhaps the sea would hold them back, then it would be split for them in the merit of Joseph — as our Rabbis of blessed memory taught (Tanhuma Beshalaḥ 9) from the verse ״הַיָּם רָאָה וַיָּנֹס״ (Psalms 114:3): what did it see? The bones of Joseph, of whom it is said ״וַיָּנָס וַיֵּצֵא הַחוּצָה״ (Genesis 39:12). And the reason for this matter we explained above in the portion of וַיֵּשֶׁב (Genesis 39:12)." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sotah 13a) expounded concerning Moses, the verse ״חֲכַם לֵב יִקַּח מִצְוֹת״ (Proverbs 10:8) — that all Israel were occupied with the spoil, while he occupied himself with the mitzvot. They sought to resolve the superfluous expression ״עִמּוֹ״, which comes to diminish the significance of acquiring the spoil of Egypt, for wealth is an acquisition that does not cleave with him, for ״לֹא יֵרֵד אַחֲרָיו כְּבוֹדוֹ״ (Psalms 49:18). But the good deeds that a person acquires in this world — that is the thing that cleaves with him in this world and in the world to come. Therefore it says ״וַיִּקַּח מֹשֶׁה אֶת עַצְמוֹת יוֹסֵף עִמּוֹ״. And perhaps it was for this reason that he engaged specifically in this mitzvah now — to serve Israel as a reminder of the day of death, for through this cause they would not be so greedily drawn after the spoil, remembering that ״יַעֲזֹב לַאֲחֵרִים חֵילוֹ״ and ״לֹא יֵרֵד אַחֲרָיו כְּבוֹדוֹ״ (Psalms 49:11, 18)."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {2 "The verse cited from Proverbs appears as ״חֲכַם לֵב יִקַּח מִצְוֹת״ which aligns with Proverbs 10:8 rather than 10:9; the text reads י (10) ט (9) which may reflect a variant verse numbering or a typographical transposition; translated the citation as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:20:04.513836555Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}